A||||4||qwikgta||Did the t-tops have any frame mods to make stronger||||1||
Z||000000||qwikgta||06-13-2001||08:58 PM||||The reason I ask is that I can't believe that the 4" of top that is left after the TTop conversion is all that is keeping the "unibody" strong.  I want to remove the top of my 87 coupe and I have been told that I need to get the "unibody" reinforced before I cut it off.  I remember that if you converted to a targa top you had to also get the frame reinforced.  How do you do it, where do they strengthen the unibody.  Anyone that has a targa have any info.  Also the convertable top that Archie bought, did he mention that it came with a "X" member for support.  <P>Thanks<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||northstar87||06-13-2001||09:09 PM||||There are no frame mods on the underside of the car.  The 2 that I own are bone stock underneath.  I hope this helps.<P>Chris Moore<BR>ASE Master Auto Tech <BR>Auto Instructor<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||06-14-2001||12:42 AM||||When t-tops are done there is a metal reinforce piece riveted to the roof between the tops. It is attatched to the windshield header and in front on the brake light. Yes that is all they get. If you are going to cut it completely you WILL need the X brace or something similar.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cozmo||06-14-2001||07:11 AM||||My targa has a reinforcing frame underneath, very similar to one of our club member's convertibles.  I don't have a pic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> but here's more or less what it looks like.  It is 1-1/2" box steel welded into a rectangle. It attaches to the inside corner of each wheelwell with a 6" extension going up into the well.  There is a removable x-frame made of the same material that bolts to flanges on the rectangle, which is welded to the car about every 4".  That gives you access to the fuel tank.  <BR>  Both cars exhibit "convertible shudder" and are a little more "loosy-goosy" than a normal Fiero. (ass-o-meter stuff...).  I have heard of people replacing the front to rear coolant tubes with big box steel tubes.  If I ever get around to strengthening the frame more (which I think it needs) I'll probably  go this route.<BR>   Don't let anybody fool you, simply welding a frame under a Fiero after you cut the top off is NOT going to replace the structural rigidity you've removed.<BR>   Still, I remember my old Triumph... if you watched the door and hood gap when you hit some bad bumps, it would vary by up to 3/4 inch! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>The targa is nowhere near that bad...<BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Toddster||06-14-2001||12:29 PM||||This is going to sound extreme but I have found it to be the best way to strengthen the fram:<P>Remove the front fender and all wheel wells. Cut into the ends of the rocker panels and slide 2" tube steel down the entire length of the rocker panel. There is a lot of crap at both ends that need to be cut out with a sawzall and some patience. Then weld another section of steel (2"x2" boxed) inside the dogleg. Weld everything to the frame. You will need to drill out the seat belt anchor bolt and rocker panel attachement points. Otherwise all you have to do is stitch-up the cuts and put the panels back on. The frame will be ROCK HARD, invisible, and you won't have any ground clearance problems.||||reg||1
